
# Network Intrusion Detection Lab | Suricata IDS

A hands-on cybersecurity lab demonstrating network traffic monitoring, custom intrusion detection rules, and suspicious activity detection using Suricata IDS.

## Project Overview

Built an isolated two-VM network using VMware Fusion and Ubuntu Server 24.04 LTS. Configured Suricata IDS to monitor network traffic and detect ICMP Echo Requests, TCP connection attempts, and potential port-scanning activity generated using Nmap.

## Technologies

- **Virtualization:** VMware Fusion
- **Operating System:** Ubuntu Server 24.04 LTS
- **Security:** Suricata IDS 7.0.3
- **Network Scanning:** Nmap
- **Protocols:** TCP/IP, ICMP

## Network Topology

| Machine | IP Address | Role |
|---|---|---|
| Ubuntu Server | 172.16.60.128 | IDS / Monitored Host |
| Test Client | 172.16.60.129 | Traffic Generator |

**Network:** `172.16.60.0/24` (Host-only)

## Detection Results

| Detection Rule | Signature ID | Result |
|---|---|---|
| ICMP Echo Request | 1000001 | Successful |
| TCP Connection Attempt | 1000002 | Successful |
| Possible TCP Port Scan | 1000003 | Successful |

The Nmap scan identified **SSH (port 22) as open**, with 999 other scanned TCP ports closed.

Suricata successfully generated alerts for all three detection scenarios. Threshold tuning reduced repeated port-scan alerts during subsequent testing.

